# Agience Origin — configuration template. Copy to `.env`.
#
# `config.load_env()` reads it at startup with `override=False`, so this file supplies DEFAULTS and
# the process environment always wins. A supervisor that sets the environment directly and ships no
# `.env` makes this a no-op.
#
# This file describes Origin and nothing else. Edge TLS, DNS credentials and host access belong to
# the reverse proxy and the host, not to this process.


# ═══ What Origin cannot boot without ═════════════════════════════════════════

# Where key material lives. AN EMPTY DIRECTORY DOES NOT BOOT. `main.py`'s lifespan calls
# `init_jwt_keys`, `init_encryption_key` and `init_nonce_secret` before it will serve anything, and
# every loader in `prism.trust.key_manager` raises rather than inventing a key it did not write —
# correct for an authority: a service that generated its own signing key at boot would silently
# become a different issuer, and one that generated its own `encryption.key` would re-key every
# secret it holds.
#
# ORIGIN SHIPS NO KEY GENERATOR. The four files below come from the platform installer
# (`agience-observe`), a KMS, or a one-shot key initialisation step:
#
#     origin.private.pem     signs every user token, delegation and service JWT (kid `origin-1`)
#     origin.public.pem      the half peers verify against — `origin-emit-anchor` reads this one
#     encryption.key         Fernet key for secrets and platform settings at rest
#     inbound_nonce.secret   HMAC secret behind the inbound-nonce gate
#
# Two more are optional, and their absence is a state rather than a fault: `platform_internal.secret`
# (missing ⇒ session cookies are signed with a per-process random key and reset on every restart,
# logged as a warning), and `setup.token` (missing ⇒ the setup wizard is already complete).
#
# GIVE EVERY INSTALL ITS OWN DIRECTORY. `encryption.key`, `inbound_nonce.secret` and
# `authority.manifest.json` carry no component name and are what every Agience component's init
# writes. Two installs sharing one directory overwrite each other, and the damage is one-sided: a
# replaced signing key costs a restart, a replaced `encryption.key` makes every stored secret
# permanently unreadable. Namespace the leaf — `~/.agience/_secrets/origin`, not a shared parent.
#
# Unset, this defaults to `<AGIENCE_BASE_DIR>/.data/keys` — derived from the install root, so it
# does not follow the directory you started the process from.
KEYS_DIR=./.data/keys

# Origin's own public address. It is the OIDC `issuer`, the base of every redirect the authority
# builds, and the fallback for AUTHORITY_ISSUER below. In-code default: http://localhost:8080
ORIGIN_URI=http://localhost:8080


# ═══ Identity and trust ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# What Origin stamps as `iss` on every token, and what peer services validate against.
# Scheme+host+port only — any path is stripped, because a path prefix must never appear in `iss`.
#
# Separate from ORIGIN_URI on purpose: peers reach Origin over an internal address
# (`http://origin:8080`) while browsers and tokens name the public one. Set this to the PUBLIC
# issuer on every service in the mesh; leave it unset only where the two coincide, as in
# single-host development. In-code default: ORIGIN_URI.
#AUTHORITY_ISSUER=

# Which browser origins Origin answers cross-site. Comma-separated, exact scheme+host+port each;
# any path is stripped, because a browser's `Origin` header never carries one and an entry with a
# path could never match.
#
# Unset means the first-party surfaces this file already declares: AUTHORITY_ISSUER, ORIGIN_URI,
# FACET_URI and every entry of FACET_URIS. Those are the pages `is_client_redirect_allowed` will
# already return a sign-in to, so they are the pages Origin answers XHR for. In development that
# resolves to the localhost defaults and nothing has to be set.
#
# SET THIS IF A FACET IS CONFIGURED ONLY IN THE SETTINGS STORE. `branding.facet_uri` and
# `branding.facet_uris` are also platform settings, loaded from the database inside the lifespan —
# after the middleware has been built and its list frozen. A facet that exists only as a stored
# setting is not in the derived default. The effective list is logged at every boot
# (`Origin: CORS allow_origins=[…]`), which is where to check.
#ORIGIN_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=https://my.example.com,https://app.example.com

# Where Origin's identity state lives (SQLite; alembic runs to head at every boot).
# In-code default: `<AGIENCE_BASE_DIR>/.data/origin/origin.db` — derived from the install root, the
# same root KEYS_DIR hangs off, so it does not follow the directory you started the process from.
#ORIGIN_DB_PATH=./.data/origin/origin.db
# The whole SQLAlchemy URL, when a path is not enough. Wins over ORIGIN_DB_PATH.
#DATABASE_URL=

# The first-party OAuth client. In-code default: `agience-client`.
#PLATFORM_CLIENT_ID=agience-client
# EVERY CLIENT ID NOT LISTED HERE (or equal to PLATFORM_CLIENT_ID) IS THIRD PARTY and receives the
# scoped, PII-free `mcp_client` token: no email, no name, no picture, no roles. A client that
# registered itself is third party too — no first-party check consults that registry — so this list
# IS the first-party test.
#
# It is also the admission list. /auth/authorize refuses any client_id that is neither listed here
# (or equal to PLATFORM_CLIENT_ID) nor registered through POST /auth/register, and refuses it
# without redirecting: an unrecognised client gets a 400 carrying `"error": "invalid_client"` and a
# description naming this setting, because the redirect target is exactly what is not trusted yet.
#
# A peer service's browser sign-in belongs here. It identifies as its own OIDC client id, which is
# configured on that service and is under no obligation to equal PLATFORM_CLIENT_ID — set that exact
# value here, or its sign-in is refused at the login page.
#
# Comma-separated; added to PLATFORM_CLIENT_ID, which needs no entry of its own.
#PLATFORM_CLIENT_IDS=

# Whether a client may enrol itself (RFC 7591 dynamic client registration, `POST /auth/register`).
# In-code default: false — no `registration_endpoint` in the discovery document, and the path
# answers 403 to anyone who finds it anyway.
#
# Turn this on and an MCP client can connect without a hand-minted token; leave it off and it
# cannot. Such clients register themselves: they read `registration_endpoint`, POST their redirect
# URIs, and use the `client_id` they get back to start the flow. With no such endpoint they never
# reach /authorize, and the only working path is a static Authorization header carrying a token an
# operator re-mints by hand.
#
# ENABLED WITHOUT A TOKEN BELOW IS OPEN REGISTRATION — anyone who can reach this path writes a row.
# On a private node that is the point. On anything reachable from the internet it is a spam surface
# with no rate limit in front of it, and the token below is the answer.
#
# ENVIRONMENT ONLY, unlike most of this file. These two are not platform settings: nothing loads
# them from the database, so a settings row by any name will not reach the running value.
#
# What a registration may promise is bounded by FACET_URI/FACET_URIS/ORIGIN_URI and loopback,
# exactly as /authorize is — a redirect URI the sign-in would refuse cannot be registered.
#
# A REGISTRATION IS BINDING, AND TURNING THIS BACK OFF DOES NOT UNDO ONE. /auth/authorize admits a
# registered client and delivers its code only to a redirect URI from its own registration; the
# allow-list above bounds what may be registered and entitles no client to another client's
# callback. This setting governs whether new rows can be written, not whether existing rows are
# read: revoking a client means deleting its row from `oauth_clients`.
#
# LOOPBACK PORTS ARE NOT PART OF THE MATCH (RFC 8252 §7.3). A native client asks the OS for a free
# port each run, so a registered `http://127.0.0.1:54321/callback` admits the same path on whatever
# port that client binds next time. Scheme, host, path and query still match exactly, and the
# relaxation applies to loopback alone — a registered https facet URI is matched with its port.
#CLIENT_REGISTRATION_ENABLED=false

# The RFC 7591 §3 initial access token. Set it and a registration must present it as a bearer token;
# leave it empty and an enabled endpoint is open. Any opaque high-entropy string (for example
# `openssl rand -base64 32`); hand it to the operator of each client that is meant to enrol.
# It is a shared secret with no expiry and no revocation list: changing it here is the only way to
# withdraw it, and doing so withdraws it from everyone at once. Clients already registered are
# unaffected — they hold a client_id, not this.
#CLIENT_REGISTRATION_INITIAL_ACCESS_TOKEN=

# Whether the internal person lookup refuses a caller that does not say on whose behalf it asks.
#
# `GET /internal/persons/{id}` is how the other services read a person's email, name and linked
# identities. It admits only the four named platform services — but a service JWT names a service
# and nothing else, so any of the four can read any person, and the request records no subject. The
# stricter form is the one `/internal/delegation-token` already uses: the caller forwards the
# person's own Origin token in an `X-Subject-Token` header, and Origin checks that the token's
# subject is the person in the path.
#
# TURN THIS ON AND A CALLER THAT SENDS NO `X-Subject-Token` GETS 403. Every service that reads a
# person must be forwarding the user's token first, or the feature behind it stops. It is off by
# default because at least one path — mail addressed from a billing webhook — resolves a recipient
# where no user token exists to forward.
#
# OFF IS NOT "UNENFORCED". A caller that sends the header is held to the full rule either way: the
# token must verify, must be a user token rather than a service or delegation one, and must name the
# person being read — a mismatch is 403 whatever this is set to. What the setting governs is the
# caller that sends nothing.
#
# THIS IS THE LOG THAT TELLS YOU WHEN TO FLIP IT. While off, every subject-less read is a WARNING
# reading `unscoped person lookup: caller=… actor=… person_id=…`. Grep it for `caller=` over a
# period long enough to cover the slow paths (billing webhooks run monthly); when nothing new
# appears, the flip is a no-op. Flipping it with stragglers in that log breaks exactly them.
#
# ENVIRONMENT ONLY, like the two above. Nothing loads it from the database.
#PERSON_LOOKUP_SUBJECT_TOKEN_REQUIRED=false


# ═══ The rest of the mesh ════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# Where users reach the front end. `is_client_redirect_allowed` accepts this and ORIGIN_URI as
# redirect bases. In-code default: http://localhost:8080
#FACET_URI=http://localhost:5173
# Every other facet base this authority admits. A node runs several facets, and each must be able to
# begin a sign-in; a facet missing from both this and FACET_URI has its redirect refused at the
# authorize step. Comma-separated. FACET_URI is not repeated here.
#FACET_URIS=

# The root every derived default hangs off (KEYS_DIR, the default DB path). Set by the platform
# installer; rarely set by hand.
#AGIENCE_BASE_DIR=


# ═══ Sign-in providers ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# These are also platform settings, and the environment wins.
# `main.py::_apply_db_settings_to_config` checks each variable before the stored value, but treats
# an EMPTY assignment as unset — so a stock template line (`GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=`) does not blank
# a key correctly stored in the DB. Leave a provider commented out entirely if the setup wizard
# configured it.

# GOOGLE MUST BE A **WEB APPLICATION** CLIENT WITH A SECRET, NOT A SPA. `oidc_providers.py`
# downgrades to a public client (`token_endpoint_auth_method: none`) when the secret is absent, and
# the failure surfaces as a rejected code exchange.
# THE REDIRECT URI MUST MATCH CHARACTER FOR CHARACTER on the Google client, or sign-in fails at the
# last hop — after a successful Google login — which reads to a user as "the login is broken".
#GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=
#GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=
#GOOGLE_OAUTH_REDIRECT_URI=https://origin.example.com/auth/callback

#MICROSOFT_ENTRA_TENANT=common
#MICROSOFT_ENTRA_CLIENT_ID=
#MICROSOFT_ENTRA_CLIENT_SECRET=
#MICROSOFT_ENTRA_REDIRECT_URI=

#AUTH0_DOMAIN=
#AUTH0_CLIENT_ID=
#AUTH0_CLIENT_SECRET=
#AUTH0_REDIRECT_URI=

# Any other OIDC provider, by discovery document.
#CUSTOM_OIDC_NAME=
#CUSTOM_OIDC_METADATA_URL=
#CUSTOM_OIDC_CLIENT_ID=
#CUSTOM_OIDC_CLIENT_SECRET=
#CUSTOM_OIDC_REDIRECT_URI=
#CUSTOM_OIDC_SCOPES=openid email profile


# ═══ Email — OTP, password reset, verification ═══════════════════════════════

# `smtp` | `gmail` | `resend` | `sendgrid` | `ses`. The stored `email.provider` setting wins over
# this; unset in both means Origin sends nothing and says so in the log.
#EMAIL_PROVIDER=
#PLATFORM_EMAIL_ADDRESS=

# GIVE THE MAILER ITS OWN CLIENT. `email_service._gmail_config` falls back to GOOGLE_OAUTH_* —
# client id, secret and `GOOGLE_OAUTH_REFRESH_TOKEN` — for whichever GMAIL_OAUTH_* value is empty,
# so rotating the sign-in client would take the one-time-code mail down with it. Two purposes, two
# clients, neither able to break the other. Set all three, or none.
#GMAIL_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=
#GMAIL_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=
#GMAIL_OAUTH_REFRESH_TOKEN=
# The fallback leg above, and the only place this name is read. Sign-in itself never uses it.
#GOOGLE_OAUTH_REFRESH_TOKEN=

# THE SMTP AND API-KEY VARIABLES BELOW ARE READ ONLY BY THE SETUP WIZARD, NOT BY THE SEND PATH.
# `routers/setup_router.py` uses them to pre-fill `/setup/status` and to inject a secret into
# platform settings when setup completes. `email_service._get_provider_config` then reads host,
# port, username and password from the SETTINGS STORE and never from the environment. So
# `EMAIL_PROVIDER=smtp` plus `SMTP_HOST=` in this file resolves to provider `smtp` with an empty
# host: mail fails to send on a node that looks configured. Complete the setup wizard, or write the
# `email.smtp.*` settings directly.
#SMTP_HOST=
#SMTP_PORT=587
#SMTP_USERNAME=
#SMTP_PASSWORD=
#SMTP_FROM=
#RESEND_API_KEY=
#SENDGRID_API_KEY=


# ═══ Password storage ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA256 iterations for NEW password hashes. In-code default: 600000, OWASP's current
# floor. Raising it is safe for accounts that already exist — `hash_password` writes the count into
# the stored hash and `verify_password` reads it back per hash, so an old hash keeps verifying at
# the count it was written with, and Origin re-derives it at the current cost on that account's
# next successful sign-in.
#
# This variable is the only way to change it. `auth.password.pbkdf2_iters` exists as a platform
# setting and is listed in config's setting map, but the function that applies that map
# (`load_settings_from_db`) has no caller in Origin — `main.py` runs its own
# `_apply_db_settings_to_config`, which does not name this value. A stored row is read by the setup
# surfaces and reaches the hashing path nowhere.
#
# It costs latency on every sign-in and every anti-enumeration dummy. Lower it only for a reason,
# and never below 600000 without one.
#PASSWORD_PBKDF2_ITERS=600000

# Require a new password sign-up to confirm its address before it can sign in. Disabled by default.
# The allowlist (comma-separated addresses and/or bare domains) is auto-verified and skips the
# mail. Both are also platform settings (`auth.email_verification.*`), which win at runtime.
#EMAIL_VERIFICATION_ENABLED=false
#EMAIL_VERIFICATION_ALLOWLIST=


# ═══ Token lifetime — the platform's revocation lag ══════════════════════════
#
# There is no variable here. An access token's lifetime is derived in
# `services/auth_service.access_token_ttl_hours()` from two stated bounds and is not readable from
# the environment. It is documented in this file because an operator looking for "how long is a
# session" looks here first, and because the number they find is not a session setting at all.
#
# It is how long a revoked credential keeps working. Peers verify Origin's tokens offline against
# the inline JWKS in their authority manifest and never call back — no introspection, no revocation
# check. So deleting an account here, demoting an operator, or resetting a password stops nothing
# that is already in a browser. The token stops when `exp` passes, on every service in the mesh.
#
# The window is 4 hours: half a working day, so a revoke issued at the start of one has landed by
# the middle of it. The floor under it is the hour Origin gives a `client_credentials` machine that
# can renew itself silently — a person may not be charged more often than a process that pays
# nothing.
#
# What it costs is sign-ins, not refreshes. Every grant returns a 30-day refresh token and no client
# in the fleet keeps one, so this window is the sign-in, and its end is a password, an OTP or a
# passkey. A client that started holding the refresh token is what would make this cheap enough to
# shorten further.
#
# Tokens already issued keep the lifetime they were minted with: nothing consults this value at
# verification time, so a deployment converges on a new window as the old tokens lapse.


# ═══ Paths read at boot ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# The optional YAML/JSON manifest applied to platform settings on first boot.
# MANIFEST_PATH names the file; DATA_PATH (default `/data`) is where it is looked for otherwise.
#MANIFEST_PATH=
#DATA_PATH=/data
# An optional file `/version` reads its version and build time from. Unset — the ordinary case —
# `/version` reports the installed distribution's own version instead. Set it only where a build
# stamps a revision worth reporting.
#BUILD_INFO_PATH=


# ═══ Escape hatches — development and tests ══════════════════════════════════
# Named so an operator recognises them in a log or a compose file, not so they get set on a node.

# Skip `alembic upgrade head` at boot. The schema must already be current.
#ORIGIN_SKIP_MIGRATIONS=1
# Skip loading platform settings and the manifest — config keeps its import-time defaults.
#ORIGIN_SKIP_DB_SETTINGS=1
# Stop `config.load_env` reading a `.env` at all. The suite sets it so a developer's real `.env` can
# never leak into a run: `load_dotenv` mutates `os.environ` outside monkeypatch's restore.
#AGIENCE_NO_DOTENV=1
